{
  "image_name": "0f273528c1d620c4b7d93c16a941175c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two related RNA interference routes in an animal cell, contrasting somatic and germline regulation and separating cytoplasmic events from nuclear chromatin effects. In the somatic pathway, double-stranded RNA is processed by Dicer and loading factors into small RNAs associated with RDE-1. This triggers post-transcriptional silencing in the cytoplasm and also recruits RNA-dependent RNA polymerase, producing secondary small RNAs that engage NRDE factors. These move into the nucleus, where they connect nascent transcripts to chromatin regulators, leading to histone methylation, H3K9me3, and repression of RNA polymerase II transcription.\n\nThe germline side compares responses to non-self versus self RNA. Non-self RNA is recognized by PRG-1 and amplified by RdRP into 22G-RNAs, which load onto HRDE-1. HRDE-1 then works with NRDE proteins and chromatin-modifying machinery to establish transcriptional silencing and repressive histone marks, much like the somatic nuclear branch. In contrast, self RNA associates with CSR-1, which appears to direct a distinct, protective pathway that counterbalances or prevents silencing at normal endogenous genes. Overall, the image highlights how related small-RNA systems distinguish foreign from self transcripts and either silence them post-transcriptionally and transcriptionally or preserve gene expression.",
